        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Noise levels are a concern from a crew operations, performance, and health standpoint.   On STS-40, the orbiter specification levels were exceeded due to the impacts of middeck payloads.  Noise levels were unsatisfactory in the Space Life Sciences-1 (SLS-1) Spacelab.   Current specifications do not adequately deal only with continuous noise levels, whereas the effects of intermittent noises (WCS, experiment operations, teleprinter operations, vacuum cleaner operations, voice communications, galley operations, etc.) add to the overall integrated daily noise levels.  New systems such as the RCRS (considered to be a continuous source) and the orbiter galley may strongly influence total orbiter noise levels.  Data taken on middeck payloads will aid in assessing the adequacy of the payload testing and specifications, and the payload effects on the overall crew compartment noise.  Data taken on the hard sleep station (bunk) will allow determination of noise isolation obtained in the bunk and potential sleep disturbance which is a concern especially on long-duration missions.
<p>
Specific objectives:<br>
1.   Obtain baseline data of octave band acoustical noise levels for the middeck and flight deck.<br>
2.     Obtain data on the effects of middeck payloads.<br>
3.     Obtain octave band data on exercise equipment (ergometer, treadmill, or rower).<br>
4.     Obtain data inside the four-tier sleep station to determine noise isolation of this station for nominal and payload operations and exercise equipment.<br>
5.     Obtain middeck acoustic data effects of new Regenerable C02 Removal System (RCRS), new galley, and new Waste Collection System (WCS), when flown.<br>
6.     Obtain manned laboratory data when labs are flown.<br>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Prior to flight, 1-hour of desk top training was given to assigned crewmembers.  During the mission, after the second sleep period, assigned crewmembers unstowed the sound level meter from stowage and took octave band, dBA, and overall dB readings at the following locations: middeck center, 1-foot from noise producing payloads, flight deck areas, and in two of the four bunks when a four-tier sleep station was flown.  Also, measured octave band levels when the following are operated:  New Shuttle Orbiter Repackaged Galley (SORG), RCRS, and the WCS or the new WCS DTO separator, if flown.  Only one set of data was taken for each different significant noise generator/configuration.   Several octave band readings were taken in the middeck when noise producing payloads were not operating or were turned off for no more than five minutes with customer's concurrence.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Results aren’t available.
<p>
